# Parts of the body, what hurts, and the doctor's advice.
lesson language-the-doctor v=1 format=teach grade=6 unit=6 subject=language levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "At the doctor"
  goal "Name parts of the body in [[language.name]], say what hurts, and understand a doctor's advice."

  look {
    scene 12x10 {
      column all at=canvas(1, 1) space=1 align=start {
        row clinic space=2 align=end {
          person doctor pose=stand age=grownup hair=bun top=white
          person child pose=sit age=child hair=short top=sky mood=sad
        }
      }
    }
    say """
      The doctor asks [[phrase.what-is-the-matter]], what is the matter? Leo answers
      [[body.my-head-hurts]], my head hurts. For more than one, [[body.my-feet-hurt]], my feet hurt.
      The doctor gives advice with [[body.you-must]]: [[body.you-must-rest]], you must rest.
      """
  }
  do {
    level easy {
      worked language.the-doctor-name k=4
    }
    show language.the-doctor-name k=2 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.the-doctor-meaning k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=4
    show language.the-doctor-write k=3 easy-k=1 hard-k=5
    show language.the-doctor-name k=4 easy-k=1 hard-k=6
    show language.the-doctor-match k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    show language.the-doctor-write k=4 easy-k=3 hard-k=6
    show language.the-doctor-listen k=3 easy-k=0 hard-k=5
    show language.the-doctor-say k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
    level hard {
      show language.the-doctor-write k=4
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show language.the-doctor-why k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show language.the-doctor-puzzle k=1 easy-k=0 hard-k=2
  }
  remember {
    say "[[phrase.what-is-the-matter]] What is the matter? [[body.my-head-hurts]] My head hurts. [[body.you-must-rest]] You must rest."
  }

  grown-ups """
    The wrong idea to watch for is two nearby parts of the body taken for each other, the head and
    the throat, the arm and the leg: each naming question describes what happened and offers the
    part most often mixed up. In [[language.name]] what hurts is said with [[body.it-hurts-me]], and
    with [[body.they-hurt-me]] for more than one, which is why the feet are said differently; a
    language without this says them the same way. The step up from the creatures of grade four,
    which had eyes and legs, is the human body and saying what is wrong with it, then understanding
    advice. From this grade the written words are marked with their accents: a word typed without
    its accent is not taken. The dictation is read from this sheet, where it prints as Read aloud,
    or played by the device. If the child is stuck, play a game of touching each part as it is
    named. Off the page, play doctors with a toy, in the language. It meets England's key stage 2
    languages programme (years 3 to 6, statutory from age 7): "describe people, places, things and
    actions orally and in writing"; Japan's grades 5 and 6 foreign languages: "Can give
    instructions, make requests and respond to them by using basic expressions"; and CEFR A2, "Can
    describe in simple terms aspects of his/her background, immediate environment and matters in
    areas of immediate need". The phrases are in [[language.variety]].
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level [[body.my-leg-hurts]] is worked first in the teacher's pen, the words to name are [[body.my-head-hurts]] and [[body.my-throat-hurts]] and the words to write are [[body.arm]] and [[body.hand]], the plainer ones of the lesson, with the first set to match, the shortest sentence to hear and the first version of each star question."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the words to name are [[body.my-arm-hurts]] and [[body.my-feet-hurt]] and the words to write are [[body.throat]], [[body.stomach]] and [[body.back]], the ones most often mixed up, with the last set to match, the longest sentence to hear, a third word to write and the last version of each star question."
  }
}
